[HELLY SIGHS]

250
00:21:08,294 --> 00:21:10,212
Thank you for taking the welcome survey.

251
00:21:10,296 --> 00:21:14,300
I can sense that the questions
made you feel afraid or disoriented.

252
00:21:14,384 --> 00:21:18,721
Well, the good news is,
you're at an orientation.

253
00:21:25,854 --> 00:21:27,981
You see, you have been hired to a position

254
00:21:28,065 --> 00:21:31,110
on the severed floor of Lumon Industries.

255
00:21:31,735 --> 00:21:32,862
The what floor?

256
00:21:43,831 --> 00:21:48,586
Ah. Uh, I understand you're confused
about the severance procedure.

257
00:21:49,128 --> 00:21:50,879
Before we hop on that,

258
00:21:50,963 --> 00:21:55,634
let's chat about something I bet you
have heard of, the work/life balance.

259
00:21:56,594 --> 00:21:58,679
To start, imagine yourself as a seesaw.

260
00:22:00,222 --> 00:22:02,557
Ow! Fuck!

261
00:22:02,641 --> 00:22:04,726
MARK: That locks from out there!

262
00:22:04,810 --> 00:22:06,186
-Let me out!
-God!

263
00:22:08,355 --> 00:22:12,234
-Can we just take a beat? Please.
-[DOORKNOB RATTLES]

264
00:22:12,318 --> 00:22:14,320
[PANTING]

265
00:22:24,330 --> 00:22:25,331
Please.

266
00:22:36,759 --> 00:22:37,760
[SIGHS]

267
00:22:39,094 --> 00:22:40,095
[SNIFFLES]

268
00:22:47,436 --> 00:22:49,230
Okay, my name's Mark.

269
00:22:50,606 --> 00:22:53,817
And, uh… So, I…

270
00:22:53,901 --> 00:22:56,403
You know, few years
back, I woke up on this table.

271
00:22:57,029 --> 00:22:58,071
In this room.

272
00:22:58,155 --> 00:23:00,365
And, uh [SMACKS LIPS]

273
00:23:00,449 --> 00:23:03,911
a disembodied voice asked
me 19 times who I was.

274
00:23:04,787 --> 00:23:06,330
[INHALES DEEPLY]

275
00:23:07,039 --> 00:23:09,249
And when I realized I couldn't answer,

276
00:23:09,333 --> 00:23:15,673
I told that voice that I would
find him and kill him. [CHUCKLES]

277
00:23:16,674 --> 00:23:19,593
I don't know why I said that.
Uh, I mean, I was scared too.

278
00:23:20,844 --> 00:23:22,179
Did you kill the voice?

279
00:23:23,889 --> 00:23:24,890
No.

280
00:23:25,599 --> 00:23:30,145
No, that voice's name was Petey,
and he became my best friend.

281
00:23:32,231 --> 00:23:35,776
So, look, you know, there
is a life to be had here, Helly.

282
00:23:36,694 --> 00:23:38,320
A life to be had?

283
00:23:41,449 --> 00:23:42,450
[BREATHES HEAVILY]

284
00:23:47,372 --> 00:23:53,837
Well, you see, life, like a seesaw, requi…

285
00:23:56,590 --> 00:23:58,341
Let me the fuck out of here!

286
00:23:58,425 --> 00:24:00,135
[PANTS]

287
00:24:07,976 --> 00:24:10,853
-MARK: <i>Ask me again.
-What?</i>

288
00:24:10,937 --> 00:24:14,858
You've asked twice to be let go.
Ask one more time.

289
00:24:16,359 --> 00:24:17,903
-Mark.
-Yeah?

290
00:24:18,987 --> 00:24:21,281
I would like to leave the building now.

291
00:24:23,700 --> 00:24:26,702
I understand you don't feel it's
working out for you here at Lumon.

292
00:24:26,786 --> 00:24:30,373
While disappointed, I'd hate to keep
you somewhere you're not happy.

293
00:24:30,457 --> 00:24:33,418
So, let's get you outta here.

294
00:24:39,883 --> 00:24:40,884
Irv.

295
00:25:03,657 --> 00:25:07,452
The departments are pretty
spaced out, but it'll all fill in one day.

296
00:25:07,536 --> 00:25:09,246
They're planning an expansion.

297
00:25:10,080 --> 00:25:11,665
And I'm, what, part of that?

298
00:25:12,499 --> 00:25:14,459
No. You're a replacement.

299
00:25:15,252 --> 00:25:16,545
Replacement for who?

300
00:25:18,421 --> 00:25:20,471
Why are you saying that like you hate it?

301
00:25:26,972 --> 00:25:29,822
[CLICKS TONGUE] Here's your stairwell.
Just past the bend.

302
00:25:37,023 --> 00:25:38,191
You're not coming with me?

303
00:25:38,275 --> 00:25:39,692
Oh, I'm not allowed to see.

304
00:25:39,776 --> 00:25:41,277
Excuse me?

305
00:25:41,361 --> 00:25:42,779
Well, once you leave, you…

306
00:25:44,531 --> 00:25:45,981
I just can't watch you leave.

307
00:25:48,618 --> 00:25:49,953
[CHUCKLES]

308
00:25:53,039 --> 00:25:54,416
Of course you can't.

309
00:26:27,575 --> 00:26:29,619
What the hell?

310
00:26:50,681 --> 00:26:52,183
[SIGHS]

311
00:26:56,729 --> 00:26:58,564
[SHOUTS, GROANS]

312
00:27:21,921 --> 00:27:23,506
-Am I dead?
-No.

313
00:27:24,131 --> 00:27:26,509
-This isn't, like, hell or something?
-No.

314
00:27:27,677 --> 00:27:29,386
Then why the fuck can't I leave?

315
00:27:29,470 --> 00:27:32,264
Well, you did leave, just now.
Out into the stairwell, at least.

316
00:27:32,348 --> 00:27:34,725
You left, but you came back.

317
00:27:35,810 --> 00:27:37,395
-I did not.
-You did.

318
00:27:38,354 --> 00:27:39,355
Come on.

319
00:27:41,691 --> 00:27:42,691
[SIGHS]

320
00:27:42,775 --> 00:27:45,695
MS. COBEL: <i>Weaponizing
office equipment on your first day.</i>

321
00:27:46,863 --> 00:27:49,156
You are gonna be fun.

322
00:27:49,866 --> 00:27:51,700
Look, I do sympathize.

323
00:27:51,784 --> 00:27:56,789
I've wanted to pummel Mark
myself, but I am his employer.

324
00:27:56,873 --> 00:27:58,916
And he is your department chief.

325
00:27:59,709 --> 00:28:02,295
So we'll both have to be strong.

326
00:28:03,629 --> 00:28:07,132
The good news is, there's only
one part left of your orientation,

327
00:28:07,216 --> 00:28:10,344
which Mark can't possibly derail.

328
00:28:11,137 --> 00:28:12,389
Why is that?

329
00:28:12,973 --> 00:28:14,892
Because it's a video.

330
00:28:22,399 --> 00:28:24,568
Welcome to Lumon, Helly.

331
00:28:50,469 --> 00:28:51,762
MS. COBEL: Have a seat.

332
00:29:10,739 --> 00:29:12,115
Are you mad at me?

333
00:29:13,283 --> 00:29:17,663
For the incompetence or the disobedience?

334
00:29:18,872 --> 00:29:20,707
-Well…
-Yes!

335
00:29:22,501 --> 00:29:24,878
You know, my mother was an atheist.

336
00:29:25,837 --> 00:29:29,216
She used to say that there was
good news and bad news about hell.

337
00:29:29,800 --> 00:29:31,218
The good news is,

338
00:29:31,885 --> 00:29:36,723
hell is just the product of a
morbid human imagination.

339
00:29:36,807 --> 00:29:37,808
The bad news is,

340
00:29:39,393 --> 00:29:43,313
whatever humans can
imagine, they can usually create.

341
00:29:47,651 --> 00:29:49,194
I don't know what that means.

342
00:29:51,071 --> 00:29:54,658
A department like yours
can go so good or so bad.

343
00:29:55,409 --> 00:29:57,327
You know what makes the difference?

344
00:30:04,209 --> 00:30:05,210
The people.

345
00:30:16,722 --> 00:30:18,422
MR. MILCHICK: You can have a seat.

346
00:30:31,946 --> 00:30:34,574
MR. MILCHICK: <i>Okay,
go ahead and have a seat.</i>

347
00:30:44,375 --> 00:30:45,460
<i>My name is Helly R.</i>

348
00:30:47,795 --> 00:30:48,963
<i>I'm making this video</i>

349
00:30:49,047 --> 00:30:53,218
<i>roughly two hours before
it will be shown to me.</i>

350
00:30:55,178 --> 00:30:58,514
<i>I have, of my own free accord,
elected to undergo the procedure</i>

351
00:30:58,598 --> 00:31:00,516
<i>colloquially known as severance.</i>

352
00:31:02,352 --> 00:31:07,439
<i>I give consent for my perceptual
chronologies to be surgically split,</i>

353
00:31:07,523 --> 00:31:11,694
<i>separating my memories between
my work life and my personal life.</i>

354
00:31:12,654 --> 00:31:13,988
<i>I acknowledge that, henceforth,</i>

355
00:31:14,072 --> 00:31:18,159
<i>my access to my memories
will be spatially dictated.</i>

356
00:31:19,452 --> 00:31:21,996
<i>I will be unable to access
outside recollections</i>

357
00:31:22,080 --> 00:31:24,999
<i>whilst on Lumon's severed basement floor,</i>

358
00:31:25,083 --> 00:31:27,544
<i>nor retain work memories upon my ascent.</i>

359
00:31:29,963 --> 00:31:33,215
<i>I am aware that this alteration</i>

360
00:31:33,299 --> 00:31:36,219
<i>is comprehensive and irreversible.</i>

361
00:31:37,804 --> 00:31:39,472
<i>I make these statements freely.</i>

362
00:31:44,811 --> 00:31:47,730
MR. MILCHICK: <i>Okay, that's a wrap.</i>

363
00:31:54,862 --> 00:31:55,863
Okay.

364
00:31:56,948 --> 00:31:57,949
Go ahead.

365
00:32:16,801 --> 00:32:18,303
So I'll never leave here?

366
00:32:20,555 --> 00:32:21,848
You'll leave at 5:00.

367
00:32:22,348 --> 00:32:25,518
Well, actually, they
stagger our exits, so 5:15.

368
00:32:25,602 --> 00:32:26,644
Hmm.

369
00:32:26,728 --> 00:32:29,898
But it won't feel like it.
Not to this version of you anyway.

370
00:32:31,608 --> 00:32:32,942
Do I have a family?

371
00:32:33,985 --> 00:32:35,320
You'll never know.

372
00:32:36,905 --> 00:32:38,615
And I have no choice.

373
00:32:39,782 --> 00:32:45,039
Well, every time you find yourself here,
it's because you chose to come back.

374
00:32:49,460 --> 00:32:50,461
[HELLY SCOFFS]

375
00:33:13,108 --> 00:33:14,109
Hello.

376
00:33:27,206 --> 00:33:28,290
[BEEPS]

377
00:33:38,926 --> 00:33:39,926
[ELEVATOR BELL DINGS]

378
00:33:40,010 --> 00:33:42,388
[HIGH-PITCHED RINGING]

379
00:33:43,639 --> 00:33:44,689
[ELEVATOR BELL DINGS]

380
00:33:52,231 --> 00:33:53,356
Good night.

381
00:33:53,440 --> 00:33:54,441
Good night.

382
00:34:43,490 --> 00:34:46,076
MARK: "Dear Mark, Whilst
carrying boxes in a room today,

383
00:34:46,160 --> 00:34:48,537
"you slipped on an
overhead projector slide"

384
00:34:48,621 --> 00:34:52,208
and sustained a minor blow to the temple."

385
00:35:20,320 --> 00:35:21,529
HELLY: Hey!

386
00:35:22,197 --> 00:35:23,782
Sorry.

387
00:35:25,158 --> 00:35:27,008
Maybe keep your eyes on the icy road.

388
00:35:29,871 --> 00:35:30,872
Sorry.

389
00:36:21,131 --> 00:36:22,132
[KEYS CLANK]

390
00:36:30,599 --> 00:36:34,603
TV NARRATOR: <i>Indeed, this gnat will
spend much of its three to seven-day life…</i>

391
00:36:41,234 --> 00:36:42,318
[SIGHS]

392
00:36:42,402 --> 00:36:45,030
[TV PROGRAM CONTINUES]

393
00:36:52,537 --> 00:36:56,958
MARK: <i>Uh-huh. No, I know, Mrs. Selvig.
It's just, it was in my space again.</i>

394
00:36:57,792 --> 00:37:01,546
Yeah. Well, trash comes tomorrow night.
Tonight's recycling.

395
00:37:02,547 --> 00:37:05,675
Yeah, it is weird, but
that's how they do it.

396
00:37:05,759 --> 00:37:08,094
And with both yours, there's no room for…

397
00:37:09,054 --> 00:37:10,055
Uh-huh.

398
00:37:14,644 --> 00:37:16,478
[TV PROGRAM PLAYING]

399
00:37:16,562 --> 00:37:17,563
[REMOTE CLICKS]

400
00:37:18,898 --> 00:37:20,274
[KNOCKS]

401
00:37:25,780 --> 00:37:27,031
-Hey.
-Hey.

402
00:37:31,244 --> 00:37:32,245
Did you forget?

403
00:37:37,208 --> 00:37:38,792
Oh, yeah. Shit.

404
00:37:38,876 --> 00:37:39,877
That's okay.

405
00:37:40,878 --> 00:37:41,879
-Sorry.
-That's fine.

406
00:37:41,963 --> 00:37:44,673
-Do you wanna put on pants, and I'll…
-I'm wearing pants.

407
00:37:44,757 --> 00:37:46,557
…meet you in… Those are not pants.

408
00:37:47,927 --> 00:37:51,763
So if there's no dinner, how is it dinner?

409
00:37:51,847 --> 00:37:52,848
-Okay.
-Like…

410
00:37:52,932 --> 00:37:55,350
You… You actually owe me this.

411
00:37:55,434 --> 00:37:56,977
-Do I?
-Yep.

412
00:37:57,061 --> 00:38:00,355
You remember that time when we were kids
and I was a way better sibling than you?

413
00:38:00,439 --> 00:38:02,900
-Oh, must have slipped my mind.
-[CHUCKLES]

414
00:38:04,360 --> 00:38:06,778
Also, I know we're
nearing the anniversary,

415
00:38:06,862 --> 00:38:09,462
so I just thought maybe
you'd wanna be around people.

416
00:38:10,157 --> 00:38:11,158
Nope.

417
00:38:13,202 --> 00:38:15,496
This is nice. When did you get it?

418
00:38:15,580 --> 00:38:17,998
Something from work, and they apologized.

419
00:38:18,082 --> 00:38:19,834
Oh, they apologized. Thank God.

420
00:38:20,877 --> 00:38:22,795
Did they tack an explanation
onto that apology?

421
00:38:22,879 --> 00:38:25,214
It's fine. Got a gift card.

422
00:38:25,298 --> 00:38:28,509
Oh, you got a gift card.
Where's he going to?

423
00:38:29,552 --> 00:38:30,552
I don't know. Pip's.

424
00:38:30,636 --> 00:38:34,181
[CHUCKLES] Of course it's Pip's.
Well, enjoy.

425
00:38:34,265 --> 00:38:36,058
MARK: I will, without you.

426
00:38:42,732 --> 00:38:46,777
[CHUCKLES] There's our captive.

427
00:38:46,861 --> 00:38:49,280
-Hi, Rick.
-Ricken. Come on, it's Ricken.

428
00:38:49,947 --> 00:38:52,450
-[GROANS] You, sir, look at peace.
-You know.

429
00:38:53,159 --> 00:38:54,368
-MARK: Okay.
-SIBLING: Hi, baby.

430
00:38:54,452 --> 00:38:56,120
-RICKEN: Hi.
-[ALL LAUGHING]

431
00:38:56,204 --> 00:39:00,791
No, what a lot of people overlook,
I think, is that life is not food.

432
00:39:00,875 --> 00:39:02,584
-Right.
-You've got life,

433
00:39:02,668 --> 00:39:06,088
this complex quality of sentience and…
and activity.

434
00:39:06,172 --> 00:39:09,675
-And then you've got food, which is what?
-Yes, what is it?

435
00:39:09,759 --> 00:39:10,968
-Fuel.
-Ah.

436
00:39:11,052 --> 00:39:13,387
-Calories. It's not the same thing.
-Mm-hmm.

437
00:39:13,471 --> 00:39:15,848
-RICKEN: Yes, that is spot on, Patton.
-FRIEND 1: I definitely agree.

438
00:39:15,932 --> 00:39:17,599
Though, if I'm… [IMITATES TAPPING ON MIC]

439
00:39:17,683 --> 00:39:22,896
Sorry, uh, Mark and my dad used to
have "whiskey is life" carved on a flask.

440
00:39:22,980 --> 00:39:24,398
[ALL LAUGHING]

441
00:39:24,482 --> 00:39:26,358
-MARK: True.
-It was low-hanging fruit.

442
00:39:26,442 --> 00:39:28,820
He did. He was a misdiagnosed alcoholic.

443
00:39:28,904 --> 00:39:32,282
Now, Mark, Ricken told Rebeck and
me that you're a former history professor.

444
00:39:32,366 --> 00:39:33,366
Yeah. Um…

445
00:39:33,450 --> 00:39:35,827
So I'm curious for
your take on all of this.

446
00:39:35,911 --> 00:39:37,162
Hmm?

447
00:39:37,246 --> 00:39:39,248
The food v life of it.

448
00:39:41,375 --> 00:39:44,919
Well, I'm not sure.
I taught mostly about World War I.

449
00:39:45,003 --> 00:39:47,130
Oh, okay. Well, I got one for you.

450
00:39:47,214 --> 00:39:48,965
I was just reading this think piece

451
00:39:49,049 --> 00:39:52,802
about the comparative levels of
violence and warfare throughout history.

452
00:39:52,886 --> 00:39:54,429
-Oh.
-Oh, nerd alert.

453
00:39:54,513 --> 00:39:56,931
War porn, I know. [CHUCKLES]

454
00:39:57,015 --> 00:40:02,479
But anyway, in it, he said that the
people actually called it the Great War.

455
00:40:02,563 --> 00:40:04,439
-Hmm.
-Apparently, it would have been a faux pas

456
00:40:04,523 --> 00:40:05,732
to have called it World War I.

457
00:40:05,816 --> 00:40:07,650
-Oh.
-RICKEN: Is that right?

458
00:40:07,734 --> 00:40:08,735
Hmm.

459
00:40:11,446 --> 00:40:16,826
Well, uh… [STAMMERS] …you know,
no one would've called it World War I

460
00:40:16,910 --> 00:40:20,246
'cause World War II hadn't happened yet.

461
00:40:20,330 --> 00:40:23,124
-FRIEND 2: [GASPS] Wow.
-RICKEN: Of course.

462
00:40:23,208 --> 00:40:24,626
-PATTON: That's absolutely right.
-Duh.

463
00:40:24,710 --> 00:40:26,377
-[CHUCKLES]
-REBECK: That's so true.

464
00:40:26,461 --> 00:40:28,005
This is why we bring this guy.

465
00:40:28,964 --> 00:40:32,550
Mark's late wife, Gemma, was an
educator as well. Russian literature.

466
00:40:32,634 --> 00:40:35,470
-REBECK: Oh, I love literature.
-RICKEN: Right? [CHUCKLES]

467
00:40:35,554 --> 00:40:37,722
But, um, Mark is a Lumon man now.

468
00:40:37,806 --> 00:40:39,140
-SIBLING: Ricken.
-Really?

469
00:40:39,224 --> 00:40:41,100
Yeah. Uh, about two years.

470
00:40:41,184 --> 00:40:43,144
-REBECK: Do you design the medicines?
-No.

471
00:40:43,228 --> 00:40:45,897
I thought Lumon was more
on the tech side of things.

472
00:40:45,981 --> 00:40:47,774
-FRIEND 2: They began in the 1800s.
-Really?

473
00:40:47,858 --> 00:40:49,651
-Topical salves, right?
-RICKEN: Hmm.

474
00:40:49,735 --> 00:40:51,694
-REBECK: What don't they make?
-[ALL CHUCKLE]

475
00:40:51,778 --> 00:40:54,489
Yeah, but I'm in the
corporate archives division, so…

476
00:40:54,573 --> 00:40:57,283
-Oh, so you know all the dirty secrets.
-[ALL CHUCKLING]

477
00:40:57,367 --> 00:40:59,160
-No, not really.
-RICKEN: No. Um,

478
00:40:59,244 --> 00:41:03,373
Mark's work is sensitive enough as
to require the severance procedure.

479
00:41:07,878 --> 00:41:08,879
FRIEND 2: Well

480
00:41:10,797 --> 00:41:12,299
that's something, isn't it?

481
00:41:14,051 --> 00:41:17,345
Yeah. It's also, I think, something
that's his choice to tell people or not.

482
00:41:17,429 --> 00:41:18,888
-Right, Ricken?
-Oh, my God.

483
00:41:18,972 --> 00:41:20,849
-No. Uh, no.
-RICKEN: Oh, God, Mark.

484
00:41:20,933 --> 00:41:22,600
-Please forgive me.
-No. It's okay. I don't care.

485
00:41:22,684 --> 00:41:25,728
-My apologies.
-No, I think it's fascinating.

486
00:41:25,812 --> 00:41:27,481
I mean, I wish I could do it.

487
00:41:28,565 --> 00:41:31,944
I'd just always be thinking
about, you know, the other one.

488
00:41:34,821 --> 00:41:37,783
Well… [STAMMERS] …there is no other one.

489
00:41:38,659 --> 00:41:40,743
It's me. I do the job.

490
00:41:40,827 --> 00:41:44,958
I just don't grasp the visceral element.
What does it feel like?

491
00:41:48,211 --> 00:41:51,589
Well, it's simple.
One's memories are bifurcated,

492
00:41:51,673 --> 00:41:55,009
so when you're not at work, you have no
recollection of what it is you do there.

493
00:41:55,093 --> 00:41:56,093
Did I get that right, Mark?

494
00:41:56,177 --> 00:41:58,971
-Well, it's…
-FRIEND 2: So you walk in at 9:00 a.m.,

495
00:41:59,055 --> 00:42:01,640
and then suddenly it's
5:00, and you're leaving?

496
00:42:01,724 --> 00:42:04,268
-Well, they stagger us a little, so…
-They stagger you?

497
00:42:04,352 --> 00:42:07,605
And then, conversely, when you're at
work, you can't access outside memories.

498
00:42:07,689 --> 00:42:11,358
So, in effect, that version
of you is trapped there.

499
00:42:11,442 --> 00:42:13,611
RICKEN: Well, uh…

500
00:42:13,695 --> 00:42:16,239
-I mean, not trapped, but…
-MARK: But what?

501
00:42:20,326 --> 00:42:24,289
No, no. I'm curious. What were you
gonna say? But not trapped, but what?

502
00:42:27,166 --> 00:42:28,216
What were you saying?

503
00:42:32,297 --> 00:42:35,090
So I suppose we know where you
fall on the congressional goings-on.

504
00:42:35,174 --> 00:42:36,467
Okay, I think we may be

505
00:42:36,551 --> 00:42:38,051
-missing the point here.
-Yeah.

506
00:42:39,345 --> 00:42:42,014
The point is that Mark made a decision.

507
00:42:42,098 --> 00:42:43,898
And that decision was controversial,

508
00:42:45,226 --> 00:42:47,186
ethically and socially.

509
00:42:47,770 --> 00:42:50,523
Morally. Scientifically.

510
00:42:51,566 --> 00:42:54,986
But, Mark, I stand behind you

511
00:42:55,612 --> 00:42:56,821
without reservation.

512
00:42:56,905 --> 00:42:58,531
-FRIEND 2: So well said.
-Thank you.

513
00:42:58,615 --> 00:43:01,265
-PATTON: Absolutely.
-I definitely stand behind Mark.

514
00:43:02,160 --> 00:43:05,079
Wow, uh… [STAMMERS] …thank you.

515
00:43:05,163 --> 00:43:07,123
That's, uh, very sweet.

516
00:43:07,999 --> 00:43:10,417
RICKEN: Well, um, we're at
the point where, traditionally,

517
00:43:10,501 --> 00:43:13,838
-I would say something like, "Dig in."
-[ALL LAUGH]

518
00:43:13,922 --> 00:43:16,507
But I must say, I do
think that the lack of food

519
00:43:16,591 --> 00:43:21,137
has allowed us to already
do so on a much deeper level.

520
00:43:21,221 --> 00:43:23,055
-PATTON: Oh, yeah. No, absolutely.
-RICKEN: Right?

521
00:43:23,139 --> 00:43:27,189
PATTON: I was saying, my friend in Lima
hasn't had a food-based dinner event in…

522
00:43:27,644 --> 00:43:30,313
SIBLING: If I give you
$3,000, will you forgive me?

523
00:43:31,606 --> 00:43:36,527
Well, because your child is
innocent of tonight's atrocities,

524
00:43:36,611 --> 00:43:40,072
I'll wait until after it's
born to murder you.

525
00:43:40,156 --> 00:43:43,576
[SIGHS] Oh, my God. Here you go, buddy.

526
00:43:46,287 --> 00:43:47,372
Mmm. Thank you.

527
00:43:52,544 --> 00:43:56,631
Hey, how's the good doctor therapy
man with that weird little mustache?

528
00:44:01,554 --> 00:44:02,555
You're not going?

529
00:44:04,390 --> 00:44:06,850
Well, the work thing's helped.

530
00:44:08,185 --> 00:44:09,186
Yeah.

531
00:44:10,771 --> 00:44:13,566
I'm proud of you for taking that job.
I really am.

532
00:44:14,775 --> 00:44:18,275
And I think she would've been too.
I know she would've been. [CHUCKLES]

533
00:44:20,364 --> 00:44:23,909
I just feel like forgetting
about her for eight hours a day

534
00:44:23,993 --> 00:44:26,829
isn't the same thing as healing.

535
00:44:34,879 --> 00:44:37,229
You wanna crash here,
since you're already here?

536
00:44:38,215 --> 00:44:39,216
No.

537
00:44:40,384 --> 00:44:41,384
Why?

538
00:44:41,468 --> 00:44:43,888
Your house smells like pregnancy.
[CHUCKLES]

539
00:44:44,388 --> 00:44:49,309
Mmm. Uh, yeah, I'm just not
grasping the visceral element of it.

540
00:44:49,393 --> 00:44:51,061
[BOTH CHUCKLING]

541
00:44:51,145 --> 00:44:52,745
-Oh, she is fun, wasn't she?
-Oh.

542
00:44:53,147 --> 00:44:55,941
-You're definitely staying here tonight.
-No, I'm not.

543
00:44:56,025 --> 00:44:57,943
RICKEN: I'm making
all the bedsheets myself,

544
00:44:58,027 --> 00:45:01,071
so I won't finish with
the big one for some time.

545
00:45:01,822 --> 00:45:04,324
But the twin is comfortable,

546
00:45:04,408 --> 00:45:07,160
and the pajamas were
made on a Baltic handloom,

547
00:45:07,244 --> 00:45:09,622
so you'll sleep well.

548
00:45:10,164 --> 00:45:14,292
Thanks.
I still don't quite get the three beds.

549
00:45:14,376 --> 00:45:16,378
-RICKEN: Right.
-Oh, Ricken's colleague told us

550
00:45:16,462 --> 00:45:20,298
that switching out the beds as the
child grows can wound that child.

551
00:45:20,382 --> 00:45:21,758
Irreparably.

552
00:45:21,842 --> 00:45:26,054
But if you provide said child
with all of its beds upon birth,

553
00:45:26,138 --> 00:45:29,183
you allow it to progress
across the room at its own rate.

554
00:45:29,808 --> 00:45:31,393
Got it. Yeah.

555
00:45:34,897 --> 00:45:37,149
RICKEN: [SIGHS] Yeah.

556
00:45:38,609 --> 00:45:40,959
I think people really
enjoyed you tonight, Mark.

557
00:45:42,863 --> 00:45:43,864
Good.

558
00:45:44,365 --> 00:45:46,616
-SIBLING: All right, baby.
-Mmm. Mm-hmm.

559
00:45:46,700 --> 00:45:47,784
-You ready, baby?
-Yeah.

560
00:45:47,868 --> 00:45:48,952
-Let's go.
-Sweet dreams.

561
00:45:49,036 --> 00:45:50,037
MARK: Thanks.

562
00:45:51,580 --> 00:45:53,081
[IN BRITISH ACCENT] Good night, milord.

563
00:45:53,165 --> 00:45:55,115
[IN BRITISH ACCENT] Good night, milady.

564
00:45:55,793 --> 00:45:56,794
[DOOR CLOSES]

565
00:46:06,095 --> 00:46:07,096
[SNIFFLES]

566
00:46:17,649 --> 00:46:18,775
[SIGHS]

567
00:47:13,955 --> 00:47:15,749
[TRAFFIC PASSING]

568
00:47:51,993 --> 00:47:52,994
Hey.

569
00:47:53,495 --> 00:47:54,496
Hey.

570
00:47:54,996 --> 00:47:56,046
How long you been up?

571
00:47:56,581 --> 00:47:57,707
A while.

572
00:48:00,961 --> 00:48:04,505
There was a businessman
in the yard last night.

573
00:48:04,589 --> 00:48:06,257
-A businessman?
-Yeah.

574
00:48:06,341 --> 00:48:08,259
Entry-level or management?

575
00:48:08,343 --> 00:48:09,344
I'm serious.

576
00:48:09,928 --> 00:48:11,637
There was seriously a
man in my yard last night?

577
00:48:11,721 --> 00:48:12,973
Yeah.

578
00:48:13,557 --> 00:48:14,558
Holy shit.

579
00:48:15,684 --> 00:48:18,603
He, you know, looked at
me weird, like he knew me.

580
00:48:18,687 --> 00:48:21,647
Aw, did the prowler invading
my home make you feel seen?

581
00:48:21,731 --> 00:48:22,899
So seen.

582
00:48:22,983 --> 00:48:23,984
[CHUCKLES]

583
00:48:24,526 --> 00:48:27,726
Well, there's a bar down the hill.
It's probably just a stumbler.

584
00:48:28,238 --> 00:48:30,950
Speaking of which, you
still smell like a distillery.

585
00:48:31,909 --> 00:48:35,370
I'm sorry, I just had to
drown out the memory

586
00:48:35,454 --> 00:48:38,540
of Mom and Dad switching
out my beds when we were kids.

587
00:48:38,624 --> 00:48:41,043
[CHUCKLING] Oh, you've
been sitting on that one.

588
00:49:25,963 --> 00:49:27,673
[CELL PHONE VIBRATING]

589
00:49:32,553 --> 00:49:33,554
Hello.

590
00:49:35,097 --> 00:49:40,060
Okay.
So, recycling was last night, Mrs. Selvig.

591
00:49:40,144 --> 00:49:41,436
Tonight's garbage.

592
00:49:41,520 --> 00:49:45,191
And you keep putting your bin on my side.
So…

593
00:49:47,193 --> 00:49:48,694
Yeah. Okay.

594
00:49:49,946 --> 00:49:53,296
You know, I actually can't talk right now.
I'm out at dinner. But…

595
00:49:54,825 --> 00:49:56,327
No, by myself.

596
00:49:58,579 --> 00:50:01,541
Uh, Pip's Bar and Grille.

597
00:50:02,583 --> 00:50:03,918
Excuse me. Wh…

598
00:50:05,253 --> 00:50:07,463
Hang up. Quickly.

599
00:50:12,552 --> 00:50:15,763
I'm sorry, Mrs. Selvig.
They've cooked the food, and it's here.

600
00:50:18,057 --> 00:50:20,977
Hi, kids. What's for dinner?

601
00:50:23,563 --> 00:50:24,564
Uh…

602
00:50:26,065 --> 00:50:28,859
I'm sorry. You don't get that reference.

603
00:50:31,279 --> 00:50:33,739
Are you following me? Wh…

604
00:50:34,740 --> 00:50:36,284
Your voice is different here.

605
00:50:37,243 --> 00:50:38,327
Worse.

606
00:50:40,413 --> 00:50:42,956
-Okay, who the fuck are you? I…
-Petey.

607
00:50:43,040 --> 00:50:45,377
My name is Petey. I'm from work.

608
00:50:47,671 --> 00:50:51,549
Well, that's not plausible.
Everyone in my department is…

609
00:50:51,633 --> 00:50:54,553
Severed. Severed. I was.

610
00:50:56,430 --> 00:50:58,598
Okay. Well, severance is pretty permanent.

611
00:50:58,682 --> 00:51:02,352
Yeah. Bypassing the implant wasn't easy.
I had help.

612
00:51:04,771 --> 00:51:07,357
Uh-huh. [SIGHS]

613
00:51:09,401 --> 00:51:10,986
[PETEY SNIFFLES]

614
00:51:15,490 --> 00:51:18,159
So, what, you were unhappy at work?

615
00:51:18,243 --> 00:51:20,829
-And instead of lodging a complaint, you…
-No.

616
00:51:21,538 --> 00:51:24,625
I tried that first. So did you.

617
00:51:26,460 --> 00:51:29,546
Right. Um, okay. So…

618
00:51:31,006 --> 00:51:32,465
You've unsevered.

619
00:51:32,549 --> 00:51:38,180
And now you, what, think they're
after you or something? Uh…

620
00:51:41,141 --> 00:51:42,142
Yeah.

621
00:51:45,479 --> 00:51:49,649
"They" being Graner, who's
probably out here right now.

622
00:51:49,733 --> 00:51:54,362
Graner. Okay.
Is that, uh, like, a person you know or…

623
00:51:54,446 --> 00:51:57,741
We both know him. We don't like him.

624
00:51:59,326 --> 00:52:00,327
I see.

625
00:52:06,250 --> 00:52:10,546
Nothing down there is what they say.

626
00:52:12,089 --> 00:52:13,799
If something happens to me,

627
00:52:14,758 --> 00:52:18,220
the things I know need to stay known.

628
00:52:20,722 --> 00:52:22,599
I'd prefer it be by a friend.

629
00:52:28,230 --> 00:52:30,732
So, we're friends?

630
00:52:32,234 --> 00:52:33,610
I'm your best friend.

631
00:52:35,279 --> 00:52:36,780
You're my very good friend.

632
00:53:07,270 --> 00:53:08,271
[MARK CHUCKLES]

633
00:53:12,150 --> 00:53:14,861
PETEY: <i>Mark, sorry about the card.</i>

634
00:53:15,904 --> 00:53:18,239
<i>I had to grab something,
and I know it's not appropriate.</i>

635
00:53:18,323 --> 00:53:21,534
<i>Though I'm sure you'd be a
really fucking awesome niece.</i>

636
00:53:23,786 --> 00:53:27,332
<i>We used to wonder what kind
of men we were on the outside,</i>

637
00:53:27,957 --> 00:53:30,668
<i>what choices we had made and why.</i>

638
00:53:37,425 --> 00:53:39,385
<i>I used to think it would take a monster</i>

639
00:53:39,469 --> 00:53:41,846
<i>to put someone in a
place like that office.</i>

640
00:53:43,640 --> 00:53:46,184
<i>Especially if the person was himself.</i>

641
00:53:49,187 --> 00:53:52,982
<i>But we're not monsters, Mark.
Not real ones.</i>

642
00:53:55,735 --> 00:53:59,781
<i>If you don't wanna know what's
going on down there, I won't force it.</i>

643
00:54:02,367 --> 00:54:06,371
<i>But if you do, there's an
address on the back of this card.</i>

644
00:54:08,373 --> 00:54:13,336
<i>Go alone, and you'll find the
beginning of a very long answer.</i>

645
00:54:31,604 --> 00:54:32,605
[SIGHS]

646
00:54:34,774 --> 00:54:36,817
NEIGHBOR: Mark, is that you?

647
00:54:36,901 --> 00:54:38,486
Oh. Hi, Mrs. Selvig.

648
00:54:39,487 --> 00:54:43,491
Oh, will you color me embarrassed
for the mix-up with the bins?

649
00:54:43,575 --> 00:54:45,618
Oh. No, it's no problem.

650
00:54:46,828 --> 00:54:47,829
How was dinner?

651
00:54:48,830 --> 00:54:53,417
Fine. I'm just, you know, tired. [SIGHS]

652
00:54:53,501 --> 00:54:56,045
You know, my mother was a Catholic.

653
00:54:56,129 --> 00:55:01,843
She used to say it takes the saints
eight hours to bless a sleeping child.

654
00:55:02,719 --> 00:55:05,096
I hope you aren't rushing the saints.

655
00:55:06,180 --> 00:55:09,601
Well, I'll give them ample time tonight.

656
00:55:11,185 --> 00:55:12,686
Good night, Mrs. Selvig.

657
00:55:12,770 --> 00:55:13,771
Mark.

658
00:55:16,942 --> 00:55:18,652
You're good people.

659
00:55:35,461 --> 00:55:36,462
[DOOR OPENS]

660
00:55:38,339 --> 00:55:39,340
[DOOR CLOSES]

